title: Getting started
tags: Documentation
kind: guide
aliases: start tutorial quickstart
summary: A first repository, a first tracked folder, a first query and a first undo with mf; then the GUI in brief.

The examples use a music folder, `~/music`, holding `albums/one.flac` and
`albums/two.flac`.

!! Before anything

From a checkout, `scripts/complete-build.sh` builds the programs into
`target/debug/` and installs the configuration under `~/.config/metafolder/`
([[Building]], [[Updating the configuration]]). Then start the daemon and leave it running:

```
metafolder-daemon
```

It listens on `127.0.0.1:7523` and loads nothing until asked ([[Daemon]]).

!! A first repository

```
mf repo init ~/music          # prints the repository's uuid
mf repo                       # the loaded repositories
```

`init` creates `~/music/.metafolder/` and loads the repository, named `music`
after its directory ([[Creating and loading a repository]]). Every command about a
repository names it before the command, with `-n <name>` ([[Global options]]).

!! Tracking the files

A new repository follows nothing: tracking is opt-in. Say that the root is watched
— it is the metarecord whose path is empty — then bring in what is already there:

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_path = ""' field set mf_watch:bool=true    # 1
mf -n music reconcile                                                     # created: 3  moved: 0
```

The first command prints how many metarecords it wrote to; the reconcile created
one metarecord for `albums` and one per file ([[Tracking a folder]]). From now on
the watcher follows the folder: a file created, renamed, edited or deleted there is
recorded a couple of seconds later, with nothing to run ([[Watcher]]).

!! Reading and writing fields

A metarecord is selected by a query (`-q`) or by its uuid (`-i`), given before the
verb ([[Selectors]]); a field is written as `name:type=value`
([[Field specifications]]).

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_path ->* "/albums"' get                    # the uuids under albums/
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_path = "/albums/one.flac"' field set rating:int=4
mf -n music metarecord -q 'rating > 3' get                                # one uuid
mf -n music metarecord -i <uuid> get                                      # the whole metarecord, as JSON
mf -n music path <uuid>                                                   # where its file is
```

`mfr_path` is the field the daemon keeps for a file's place in the tree; `rating`
is yours, as is any name outside the `mfr_` and `mf_` prefixes ([[Reserved fields]]). The query
language is described in [[Writing queries]]; a shorter form exists for everyday
use, `-s` ([[Using simplified queries]]):

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'rating>=4' -s get --count
```

!! Taking a change back

Every write is a revision of the repository's history ([[Event log]]):

```
mf -n music log               # the revisions, newest first
mf -n music log undo          # takes back the newest change you made
mf -n music log redo          # … and brings it back
```

([[Undo and redo]].)

!! The GUI in brief

```
metafolder-gui
```

The GUI connects to the running daemon ([[Connection to the daemon]]) and starts
with one empty workspace: pick a repository in the [[repos panel]], and the
workspace is on it ([[Workspaces]]).

* The window shows one or two ''panels'' side by side; each slot can show any panel type — the list of metarecords, the detail of one, the file manager, the log… ([[Panel types]], [[Panel slots and layout]]).
* Everything is a ''command'': <<key "command-input:focus command" ":">> focuses the command input, which completes command names; <<key "command-input:focus bash" "!">> runs a shell line instead ([[The command input]], [[Commands]]).
* Almost every command has a ''key'', and every key can be changed ([[Keyboard navigation]], [[Changing keybindings]]).
* Typing in the list's query box filters the metarecords, in the simplified language ([[Using simplified queries]]).
* <<key "help:open" "h h">> opens this documentation; <<key "help:cursor" "h c">> then a click opens the page of what was clicked, and <<key "help:key" "h k">> says what a key runs ([[In-app help]]).

